<section aria-label="RPG Maker MV save editing guide">
  <h2>What is an .rpgsave editor?</h2>
  <p>
    An .rpgsave editor reads a RPG Maker MV save slot and lets you change compatible values
    before rebuilding the file. SaveFileTool runs this workflow in your browser. Choose your
    desktop save above, check the detected gold value, make a small change, and download a zip
    containing the edited save and an original backup. You do not need an account or an installer.
  </p>
  <p>
    An MV save records more than money: it can contain party members, inventory, map position,
    switches, variables, and progress. Those values belong to the game's rules. A number that
    looks editable may control a quest or an event rather than a resource. Gold is the verified
    edit path here; other detected fields remain candidates until you check them in your game.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to find RPG Maker MV save files</h2>
  <p>
    For a downloaded Windows game, start in the folder containing its executable. Many packaged
    MV games put the project inside <code>www/</code>, with saves in <code>www/save/</code>.
    Others keep project files next to the executable and use <code>save/</code> directly.
    An editor test project commonly uses the project's own <code>save/</code> folder.
    Plugins and distribution choices can change these locations, so they are places to check,
    not a promise that every MV game uses the same directory.
  </p>
  <p>
    Save once in the game before searching. Look for a slot such as <code>file1.rpgsave</code>
    and compare its modification time with your latest save. Files named <code>global.rpgsave</code>
    or <code>config.rpgsave</code> serve different purposes; choose the numbered slot for changing
    party gold. If several copies exist, make a fresh save and identify which directory changes.
    Copy that file somewhere separate before opening it in the editor.
  </p>
  <p>
    A game marked “Play in browser” may store progress in browser storage instead of writing
    an .rpgsave file to disk. A desktop file picker cannot select that stored record directly.
    Use an export feature provided by the game if one exists, or consult its save instructions.
    Changing a downloaded web page's filename to <code>.rpgsave</code> does not create a save.
  </p>

  <h2>Change gold and load the edited save</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Record the starting value.</strong> Open the game's menu, note your gold, and save
      to a known slot. Close the game before replacing files so it cannot overwrite your changes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Make a separate backup.</strong> Copy the numbered .rpgsave file to another folder.
      Keep its original name so you can restore the right slot later.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Choose the file above.</strong> Confirm that the editor identifies RPG Maker MV
      and displays the gold you recorded. If the values do not match, check the slot and directory
      before editing; you may have selected an older save.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Edit one value.</strong> Enter a modest gold amount and leave other fields alone
      for the first attempt. Avoid extreme numbers or multiple changes that make a failure hard
      to diagnose.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Download and extract the zip.</strong> The package contains the rebuilt slot,
      the original backup, and instructions. Put the edited numbered file in the game's save
      directory; do not place the zip itself there or replace it with the backup-prefixed file.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Load the same slot.</strong> Reopen the game, choose Continue, and inspect the gold
      in its menu. Check your location and party as well. Keep the backup until the game loads
      correctly and normal play resumes.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What the MV gold verification covers</h2>
  <p>
    Our verification used the official RPG Maker MV 1.6.1 trial package's default project,
    running in its Windows game runtime. The game generated the original .rpgsave with
    <strong>1234 gold</strong>. The local editor changed it to <strong>98765</strong>, exported
    it, and the MV game loaded the edited file successfully. Its menu displayed the new amount.
    This checks the full save, edit, export, and game-load workflow.
  </p>
  <p>
    Comparing the decoded files showed that only the party gold field changed. Control values
    remained intact: variable 1 was 42, switch 1 was true, and item 1 had a quantity of 3.
    The exported original backup also matched the input file byte for byte. These are preservation
    checks; they do not establish that editing those other fields is safe in every game.
  </p>
  <p>
    This evidence comes from an official trial project, not a third-party commercial game.
    It supports the standard MV gold workflow. A game with custom save plugins, integrity
    checks, or a different data layout needs its own small edit and in-game loading check.
    Keep that distinction in mind when deciding whether to replace a valuable progress file.
  </p>

  <h2>MV .rpgsave and MZ .rmmzsave are different formats</h2>
  <p>
    RPG Maker MV and MZ use different save formats even when their games look similar.
    Standard MV 1.6.1 saves use LZString compression encoded as Base64. Standard MZ saves use
    a different compression workflow based on pako. Their filenames identify different engine
    formats: <code>.rpgsave</code> for MV and <code>.rmmzsave</code> for MZ. Changing the extension
    does not convert the stored data or the engine objects inside it.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page accepts .rpgsave files only. If your actual slot ends in .rmmzsave, open the
    <a href="/editor/rpg-maker-mz/">RPG Maker MZ save editor</a> instead. Older extensions such
    as <code>.rxdata</code>, <code>.rvdata</code>, and <code>.rvdata2</code> belong to other
    RPG Maker generations and are outside this page's published support. Choose the editor
    by the original file extension rather than the game's art style.
  </p>

  <h2>Why plugins and custom saves may not work</h2>
  <p>
    MV developers can install plugins that add fields, encrypt saves, change compression,
    or move data into another container. A file can still end in .rpgsave while differing from
    the default engine layout. Parsing a file successfully also does not prove that every
    displayed field has the meaning you expect, or that the game's own checks will accept
    an edited value.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a save fails to parse, try a newly created slot from the same game and confirm that
    you selected the actual save rather than an archive or asset. If the rebuilt file fails
    to load, restore your backup and retry a smaller gold-only change. Persistent failure
    suggests that this game's structure needs further investigation; repeatedly renaming
    or recompressing the file is not a reliable fix.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy, backups, and the 10 MB limit</h2>
  <p>
    Selecting a save here reads it locally in your browser. The editing workflow does not
    upload your save contents to a server. The download includes the original backup so you
    can reverse a failed change. Keep an additional copy outside the game directory as well,
    particularly if a launcher or cloud service synchronizes that folder.
  </p>
  <p>
    Files larger than 10 MB are rejected. If you selected a whole project or zip archive,
    extract it and choose the numbered save itself. An unusually large .rpgsave may contain
    plugin data; do not delete parts of the file merely to fit the limit. Optional local history
    stores copies in browser storage on your device, and it is separate from your game directory.
  </p>

  <h2>RPG Maker MV save editing questions</h2>
  <h3>Why does the game still show my old gold?</h3>
  <p>
    Check that you extracted the edited file, replaced the correct slot, and loaded that slot
    after restarting the game. Multiple installations can have separate save folders. Cloud
    synchronization or a still-running game may also restore an older copy; compare file
    modification times before trying another edit.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I open the .rpgsave file in a text editor instead?</h3>
  <p>
    A standard MV save contains compressed data, so its visible text is not ordinary editable
    JSON. Changing characters directly can damage the encoded content. Use a compatible editor
    to decode and rebuild it, and keep your original file for recovery.
  </p>
  <h3>Can this editor change quest switches or inventory safely?</h3>
  <p>
    Those fields are candidates. Their meaning and allowed values depend on the game and its
    plugins. Folder Mode can load names from the game's JSON data files to help identify IDs,
    but labels are not a compatibility guarantee. Test one small change in a disposable slot
    before editing progress you want to keep.
  </p>
  <h3>What should I do if the edited slot will not load?</h3>
  <p>
    Close the game and restore the original numbered file from your separate backup or the
    downloaded package. Check that the restored slot loads normally. Then, if you retry,
    change only gold and use a conservative value. Keep the failing edited copy separate
    from your working backup so you can compare the two.
  </p>
</section>
